<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
     FacetUI: glass dialogs.

     These three attributes have existed in the platform since Android 12 and
     are read straight off the window's theme by PhoneWindow.generateLayout():

         if (a.getBoolean(R.styleable.Window_windowBlurBehindEnabled, false)) {
             params.flags |= FLAG_BLUR_BEHIND;
             params.setBlurBehindRadius(a.getDimensionPixelSize(
                     Window_windowBlurBehindRadius, 0));
         }
         setBackgroundBlurRadius(a.getDimensionPixelSize(
                 Window_windowBackgroundBlurRadius, 0));

     No platform theme sets any of them. They are simply unused, which means a
     resource overlay can turn them on with no code at all, and every Dialog
     goes through PhoneWindow, so this reaches all of them at once.

     WHY THESE TWO STYLES, AND ONLY THESE TWO

     Overriding a style in an RRO replaces the whole style, so any attribute the
     original declared and the override does not restate is lost. That makes a
     big theme a dangerous thing to override.

     Theme.Material.Dialog and Theme.Material.Light.Dialog are, in the platform,
     completely EMPTY:

         <style name="Theme.Material.Dialog" parent="@*android:style/Theme.Material.BaseDialog"/>

     There is nothing in them to lose. Everything a dialog actually gets comes
     from BaseDialog above, which is untouched, and every dialog variant in the
     system inherits down through these two - Alert, MinWidth, NoActionBar,
     their DeviceDefault counterparts, and so on. So this is the largest reach
     available for the smallest possible risk.

     The parents are restated explicitly and must stay correct; the style
     override check in tools/validate-overlays.py enforces that.

     Two things here are namespace-qualified in ways the framework's own copy
     is not, and both are aapt2 errors if you forget:

       The parent styles take @*android:style/. They are framework styles, so
       an unqualified name resolves against THIS package, which has no such
       style. The star is the private-reference form: Theme.Material.BaseDialog
       is not in public-final.xml, so a plain @android: reference is rejected
       too.

       The four attributes take android:. Unqualified, aapt2 looks for an attr
       of that name in this package and fails. They are all public, so no star.

     None of this is visible to a resource-name check - only to a linker.
-->
<resources>

    <!-- Dark dialogs. -->
    <style name="Theme.Material.Dialog" parent="@*android:style/Theme.Material.BaseDialog">
        <!-- L2: the dialog panel itself samples what is behind it. -->
        <item name="android:windowBackgroundBlurRadius">24dp</item>

        <!-- L4: and the whole screen behind the dialog goes to the deepest
             tier, so the dialog reads as a pane held above the app rather
             than a card pasted onto it. -->
        <item name="android:windowBlurBehindEnabled">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBlurBehindRadius">64dp</item>

        <!-- Stock is 0.6 (0.7 on some DeviceDefault themes). That much black
             over a blur is just black: the dim buries the effect the two
             radii above pay for. Lowered to the point where the blur is what
             separates the dialog from the app, and the dim only deepens it. -->
        <item name="android:backgroundDimAmount">0.32</item>
    </style>

    <!-- Light dialogs. Same reasoning throughout. -->
    <style name="Theme.Material.Light.Dialog" parent="@*android:style/Theme.Material.Light.BaseDialog">
        <item name="android:windowBackgroundBlurRadius">24dp</item>
        <item name="android:windowBlurBehindEnabled">true</item>
        <item name="android:windowBlurBehindRadius">64dp</item>
        <!-- Slightly higher than the dark theme: a light panel over blurred
             content needs more separation from it to stay legible. -->
        <item name="android:backgroundDimAmount">0.36</item>
    </style>

</resources>
